# Embedded Project Development Guidelines

## Design Principles

- Follow SOLID principles: Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, Dependency Inversion
- No hardcoding: use configuration structures and dynamic registration instead of hardcoded types, names, or dispatch tables
- No magic numbers: all constants must be named
- Mutable global variables are forbidden; use context/handle structs
- `static const` read-only globals are allowed when they carry no runtime state and improve clarity

## Implementation (ESP-IDF Specific)

- Use ESP-IDF native components when available; do not reinvent what IDF already provides
- Prefer standard libraries over custom implementations
- Never invent function signatures — verify against IDF headers or official docs before writing any call
- Log errors with `ESP_LOGE`; use `ESP_LOGW` / `ESP_LOGI` for warnings and info

## Testing

- Code must compile without warnings (`-Wall -Werror` clean)
- Verify with actual ESP-IDF components, not stubs alone
- Test with working end-to-end examples
- Handle edge cases: NULL inputs, empty buffers, timeout, resource exhaustion

## Anti-Patterns

- Hardcoded lists, names, or method dispatch tables
- Magic numbers without named constants
- Inventing function signatures without verifying against IDF headers
- Automatically generating summary, changelog, or analysis documents
